{
  "image_name": "8ec7eee6808ef51938b80d969aab9c78.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several charge-storage mechanisms in battery-like cathode materials and contrasts how anions contribute to capacitance. Overall, it links the shape of a current–voltage response to different microscopic processes: ordinary electrical double-layer charging, surface redox pseudocapacitance, intercalation capacitance, self-discharge behavior, and redox-centre introduction between anode and cathode.\n\nA schematic voltammogram at the top left shows a mostly rectangular capacitive response, with a shaded region indicating capacitance associated with anions during charge and discharge around the open-circuit voltage. The structural cartoons on the right and lower panels illustrate how ions interact with host materials in distinct ways. In electrical double-layer capacitance, anions accumulate near the cathode surface without obvious insertion. In surface redox pseudocapacitance, ions appear to adsorb at the surface while electrons transfer to active sites. In intercalation capacitance, anions move into open spaces within the crystal lattice. Another panel suggests that different anion types lead to different self-discharge tendencies. The final schematic shows redox centres and cations shuttling between anode and cathode materials, emphasizing electron-transfer reactions coupled to ion motion. Together, the figure compares non-faradaic and faradaic storage pathways involving anions.",
